  "body_html": "<h1>Nash Existence For Finite Games</h1>\n<p>Every finite strategic game has a mixed equilibrium.</p>",
  "proof_html": "<p>Sketch</p>\n<p>MFoGT constructs Nash's continuous self-map $f$ on\n$$\n  \\Delta=\\prod_{i\\in I}\\Delta(S_i)\n$$\nby increasing the weight of pure strategies whose payoff exceeds the current\nexpected payoff. Brouwer's fixed point theorem gives $\\sigma=f(\\sigma)$. At such\na fixed point, no player can have a pure strategy with strictly larger payoff than\n$g_i(\\sigma)$; otherwise the normalization equation forces a contradiction for a\nstrategy in the support. Hence $\\sigma$ is a mixed Nash equilibrium.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[MFoGT, Thm. 4.6.2] Laraki, Renault, and Sorin, <em>Mathematical Foundations of Game Theory</em>, <code>references/MathFoundationsGameTheory.pdf</code>. Every finite game has a mixed equilibrium.</li>\n</ul>",
